WEBVTT

00:00:00.970 --> 00:00:26.790
<v Ira Glass>We're in Danielle's house. Ever since she was a girl, when holidays dinners come they serve a meal that will probably look familiar to you. Picture, please, a main course, big platter, drumsticks, white breast meat, golden brown skin, stuffing and gravy and cranberry relish on the side. And in Danielle's family, they have a name for this meal that she told me on the phone recently. The name for this meal is--
<v Ira Glass>我们在丹妮尔的家里。从小到大，每逢节假日晚餐，她们家端上桌的菜你可能很熟悉。请想象一下：主菜是一大盘鸡腿、白嫩的胸肉、金黄酥脆的表皮，再配上填料、肉汁和蔓越莓酱。在丹妮尔家里，这道菜有一个她在电话里告诉我的名字。这道菜的名字是——

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:27.540
<v Danielle>Fish.
<v Danielle>鱼。

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:28.040
<v Ira Glass>Got that?
<v Ira Glass>听明白了吗？

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:31.580
<v Danielle>Fish.
<v Danielle>鱼。

00:00:31.580 --> 00:01:41.880
<v Ira Glass>Well, from WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. Special program today on the wonders of fish. All right, actually we can say the word here. And the word is poultry. And, as you probably know, each week on our program, we choose some theme, invite a variety of writers and performers and documentary producers to tackle that theme. And this week, as we, all of us, stand in that magical five-week period, that magical interregnum between the turkey of Thanksgiving and the turkey of Christmas, that magical five weeks where Americans consume nearly a fourth of all the turkey we consume over the course of the year, 67 million turkeys. So in this period, we bring you our annual Poultry Slam, stories about turkeys, chickens, ducks, fowl of all kind, and their mysterious hold over us.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台的《美国生活》。今天我们要播出一期关于鱼之奇迹的特别节目。好吧，其实我们这里可以说出来。那个词是家禽。正如你所知，我们每周都会设定一个主题，邀请各类作家、表演者和纪录片制作人来探讨这个主题。本周，我们所有人正处于那个神奇的五周时期——感恩节火鸡与圣诞节火鸡之间的那个神奇间歇期，这五周里美国人消耗了全年近四分之一的火鸡，也就是6700万只。因此，在这一时期，我们为你带来一年一度的“家禽大满贯”，讲述关于火鸡、鸡、鸭和各种禽类的故事，以及它们对我们那种神秘的掌控力。

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:45.950
<v Ira Glass>Well, I'm Ira Glass. Coming up in this hour, Act One, Duki.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。接下来在本节目中，第一幕：杜基。

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:47.360
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, Turkey.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕：火鸡。

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:53.530
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, writer David Sedaris.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕：作家大卫·塞达里斯。

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:57.970
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, South African Chicken Blood.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕：南非鸡血。

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:09.130
<v Ira Glass>And of course, what poultry-positive program would be complete without--
<v Ira Glass>当然，什么样的家禽主题节目能少了它呢——

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:09.610
<v Chickenman>Chickenman!
<v Chickenman>鸡侠！

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:13.430
<v Women>He's everywhere! He's everywhere!
<v Women>他无处不在！他无处不在！

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:46.150
<v Ira Glass>Yes, indeed, in the late 1960s, the winged warrior, Chickenman, struck terror in the hearts of evildoers on radios all across our great nation. And today, we are pleased to bring you the first new episode of Chickenman since 1969. This is one of those things if you have heard Chickenman , this needs no introduction. If you haven't, you are in for a big, big treat, a big radio treat. It's later in the show. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>没错，在20世纪60年代末，这位长着翅膀的战士——鸡侠，曾在我们伟大国家的电台里让恶人们闻风丧胆。今天，我们很高兴为你带来1969年以来的第一部鸡侠新剧。如果你听过鸡侠，这无需多言；如果你没听过，那你可有福了，这是一场广播盛宴。节目稍后播出，请继续收听。

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:55.510
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Duki. So in Danielle's family, the power of poultry is so great in their lives that when they serve chicken or turkey, they call it--
<v Ira Glass>第一幕：杜基。在丹妮尔家里，家禽的力量在她们的生活中是如此巨大，以至于每当她们供应鸡肉或火鸡时，她们都管它叫——

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:56.050
<v Danielle>Fish.
<v Danielle>鱼。

00:02:56.050 --> 00:03:12.000
<v Ira Glass>That's right. And they call it that for a reason. And the reason has to do with a stuffed hand puppet called Duki. Now Danielle is nearly 30 years old. Her sister, Ashley, is two years younger. And Duki has been in the family since they were children.
<v Ira Glass>没错。她们这么叫是有原因的。这个原因与一个叫杜基的塞棉手偶有关。丹妮尔现在快30岁了。她妹妹艾什莉比她小两岁。从她们小时候起，杜基就在家里了。

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:52.950
<v Danielle>Well, he was a Christmas present when Ashley was about 8, and I was about 10. And when he first arrived, he was really fluffy. And he was this beautiful, fluffy, white duck. And he had a cape on and black, villain slash hero goggles. He lost the outfit pretty quickly, and he went naked. And then he became Ashley's vehicle for torturing me.
<v Danielle>嗯，那是艾什莉8岁、我10岁时收到的圣诞礼物。他刚到家的时候真的很毛茸茸。他是一只漂亮、毛茸茸的白鸭子。他披着斗篷，戴着黑色的反派兼英雄护目镜。他很快就弄丢了那身行头，开始“裸奔”了。后来，他成了艾什莉折磨我的工具。

00:03:52.950 --> 00:04:37.300
<v Ira Glass>Now, it's not unusual for older siblings to dominate younger ones. And as children, Danielle dominated Ashley. Ashley looked up to Danielle, fought to get her attention and her approval, and Danielle always, always got her way, except when Duki was around. Basically, Ashley would channel-- I mean the word is an anachronism in this context, but she would channel Duki. She should become Duki's voice. She would speak as Duki. And Duki was sarcastic. Duki was selfish and bossy. Duki would insult Danielle. Duki would tease Danielle. Duki would give her painful nose squeaks.
<v Ira Glass>年长者主导年幼者并非罕见。小时候，丹妮尔总是压制艾什莉。艾什莉仰慕丹妮尔，争取她的关注和认可，而丹妮尔总是能随心所欲，唯独在杜基在场时例外。基本上，艾什莉会通过——在这个语境下用“通灵”这个词或许不太恰当，但她会进入杜基的状态。她会变成杜基的嗓音，替杜基说话。杜基很刻薄。杜基很自私又爱指手画脚。杜基会侮辱丹妮尔，嘲笑丹妮尔，还会给她捏鼻子，弄得很疼。

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:47.360
<v Danielle>Whenever Ashley brought Duki into the equation, he was completely the dominant force. I was just putty in Duki's hands.
<v Danielle>每当艾什莉把杜基搬出来时，他就是绝对的支配者。在杜基手里，我简直就像橡皮泥一样。

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:53.800
<v Ira Glass>Let me ask you to compare his personality with Ashley's personality.
<v Ira Glass>我请你对比一下他的性格和艾什莉的性格。

00:04:53.800 --> 00:05:24.690
<v Danielle>Ashley's very considerate and kind and thoughtful. And very, very sensitive to other people, very, very concerned about if other people are happy, and if someone else doesn't feel good. And Duki has this total, what's for lunch attitude. Like what's in it for me? In your face, totally out for himself, simultaneously a braggart and a total wimp.
<v Danielle>艾什莉非常体贴、善良且善解人意。她对别人非常敏感，非常关心别人是否快乐，是否有人感到不舒服。而杜基完全是一副“午饭吃什么”的态度。就好像“我能从中得到什么？”他那种咄咄逼人的态度，完全是为了自己，同时既是个吹牛大王，又是个彻头彻尾的懦夫。

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:26.660
<v Ira Glass>He's boastful and vain.
<v Ira Glass>他自大又虚荣。

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:41.660
<v Danielle>He's just this indomitable spirit.
<v Danielle>他就是那种不屈不挠的“精神”。

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:55.280
<v Ira Glass>Well, 19 years after Duki arrived in the Mattoon household, the fluffy whiteness is long gone. Fluffy, in fact, is not a word you would really use to describe Duki. Nor is two-eyed, if I remember correctly.
<v Ira Glass>在杜基来到马顿家19年后，那种毛茸茸的白色早已消失不见了。事实上，如果你想形容杜基，你绝对不会用“毛茸茸”这个词。如果我没记错的话，也不会用“双眼俱全”来形容他。

00:05:55.280 --> 00:06:04.070
<v Danielle>What he looks like physically is just-- he's a slightly pathetic looking, gray, tattered thing.
<v Danielle>从外观上看，他简直就是——一个看起来有点可怜、灰扑扑、破破烂烂的东西。

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:04.500
<v Ira Glass>Very tattered.
<v Ira Glass>非常破烂。

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:30.570
<v Danielle>Very tattered. But then the fact that his brain, what's coming out of his mouth, is in complete denial about who he actually is-- I don't know. There's just something really, really great about that. And really, you have to love him for it.
<v Danielle>非常破烂。但事实是，他的大脑，或者说他嘴里说出来的东西，完全否认了他真实的样貌——我不知道。关于这一点，真的非常非常棒。你真的不得不因此而爱上他。

00:06:30.570 --> 00:07:13.800
<v Ira Glass>All right, I've been at Danielle's apartment sometimes, and I've witnessed the following scene. Picture, please. Danielle has not spoken with her sister in weeks. She picks up the phone, calls Ashley in Michigan. Ashley answers. Danielle asks immediately, "Can you put Duki on?" And then Ashley essentially becomes Duki, puts Duki on the phone. Danielle talks to Duki for 15, 20 minutes. And then they both hang up. That's the whole conversation. And they both feel satisfied. These are adults. Danielle is an editor at a big New York magazine.
<v Ira Glass>好了，我有时会去丹妮尔的公寓，亲眼见证过以下场景。请想象一下：丹妮尔已经几周没跟她妹妹说话了。她拿起电话，打给密歇根州的艾什莉。艾什莉接听后，丹妮尔直接问：“能让杜基接电话吗？”然后艾什莉基本上就变成了杜基，让杜基接听电话。丹妮尔和杜基聊上15到20分钟。然后她们挂断电话。这就是整个通话过程。她们都感到很满足。这些人可是成年人。丹妮尔还是纽约一家大杂志社的编辑。

00:07:13.800 --> 00:08:02.030
<v Danielle>I adore Duki. I really love Duki. And sometimes, I think if he disappeared, it would really feel like someone died. I mean, I look at him, and he looks really old and ratty, and it really makes me sad. I mean, it sounds crazy. It really makes me sad to think about a world without Duki in it. There would be a big, empty hole in the world. He takes up as much room in my heart as a lot of people individually. And if something happened to him, if he were lost at an airport or run over by a car, it would really be heartbreaking.
<v Danielle>我爱慕杜基。我真的爱杜基。有时候我想，如果他消失了，那种感觉就像有人去世了一样。我是说，我看着他，他看起来真的很老旧、破烂，这让我感到很难过。我是说，这听起来很疯狂。想到一个没有杜基的世界，我真的会很难过。世界上会留下一个巨大的空洞。他在我心中的位置和许多人是一样的。如果他出了什么事，比如在机场丢了，或者被车压了，那真的会让人心碎。

00:08:02.030 --> 00:08:14.190
<v Ira Glass>So I hope it's becoming clear why, if you eat dinner in the home of Danielle's family, if they're serving some kind of poultry, chicken or turkey, if you were to ask anybody in the family what's for dinner, they'll tell you--
<v Ira Glass>所以我想现在应该很清楚了，为什么如果你在丹妮尔家里吃晚饭，如果她们端上任何种类的家禽，鸡肉或火鸡，如果你问家里任何人晚饭吃什么，她们会告诉你——

00:08:14.190 --> 00:08:14.680
<v Danielle>Fish.
<v Danielle>鱼。

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:18.790
<v Ira Glass>Right. And the rationale for that is what?
<v Ira Glass>没错。那理由是什么呢？

00:08:18.790 --> 00:08:20.090
<v Danielle>It freaks Duki out.
<v Danielle>这会把杜基吓坏的。

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:25.970
<v Ira Glass>It freaks him out-- you don't like him to know that perhaps some birds are in fact eaten?
<v Ira Glass>会吓坏他——你不希望他知道可能有些鸟类实际上是被吃掉的吗？

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:42.930
<v Danielle>I think he knows. I think he's in denial about it. He's in denial about most things. He's in denial about the fact that he's totally weak and tiny and dirty. He thinks he's really good-looking and strong and that he's really smart and has a lot of friends.
<v Danielle>我想他是知道的。但我认为他在否认这件事。他否认大多数事情。他否认他其实很虚弱、渺小且肮脏的事实。他认为自己又帅又强壮，还觉得他很聪明，有很多朋友。

00:08:42.930 --> 00:08:45.770
<v Ira Glass>Right.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:08:45.770 --> 00:09:08.000
<v Danielle>He's in denial about the fact that he's actually stuffed, which he is. Sometimes I tell him that. I say, "Duki, give me a break. You're just stuffed." And he's like, "No way."
<v Danielle>他否认他实际上是塞了棉花的，但他确实是。有时候我告诉他：“杜基，放过我吧，你只是塞了棉花而已。”而他会说：“才怪。”

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:35.240
<v Ira Glass>Now, I thought I would try to book Duki to come on the radio for this program. So I contacted Danielle's sister, Ashley, and asked her, "Could Duki come on the air?" I received an answer back, not by phone, but by electronic mail, that for Duki to appear, I'd have to first go through someone named Yona Lu, who I could reach through Danielle and Ashley's mother. And when I talked to Danielle, I asked her about this.
<v Ira Glass>我想我应该试着为这档节目预约杜基上电台。所以我联系了丹妮尔的妹妹艾什莉，问她：“杜基能上节目吗？”我收到的回复不是通过电话，而是电子邮件，说如果杜基要参加，我必须先通过一个叫约娜·陆的人，我可以通过丹妮尔和艾什莉的母亲联系到她。当我跟丹妮尔谈起这件事时，我问了她。

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:42.500
<v Ira Glass>I've been informed that the only way that I can reach him is by calling your mom and speaking to Yona Lu. Do I have that name right?
<v Ira Glass>我被告知唯一能联系到他的办法是打给你妈妈，找约娜·陆谈。那个名字我没记错吧？

00:09:42.500 --> 00:09:46.250
<v Danielle>Yona Lu, yeah. I think she's acting as his agent.
<v Danielle>约娜·陆，对。我想她是在担任他的经纪人。

00:09:46.250 --> 00:09:47.610
<v Ira Glass>Yona Lu is?
<v Ira Glass>约娜·陆是吗？

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:51.780
<v Danielle>She's a hedgehog.
<v Danielle>她是一只刺猬。

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:56.880
<v Ira Glass>Anything special that I should say to Yona Lu to make this happen?
<v Ira Glass>有什么特别的话是我该对约娜·陆说，好让她促成这件事吗？

00:09:56.880 --> 00:10:02.400
<v Danielle>I don't know. She drives a pretty hard bargain. [PHONE RINGING]
<v Danielle>我不知道。她可是个很难对付的谈判者。[电话铃响]

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:03.150
<v Mrs. Mattoon>Hello?
<v Mrs. Mattoon>喂？

00:10:03.150 --> 00:10:03.530
<v Ira Glass>Hey, Mrs. Mattoon.
<v Ira Glass>嘿，马顿太太。

00:10:03.530 --> 00:10:04.270
<v Mrs. Mattoon>Yes?
<v Mrs. Mattoon>你好？

00:10:04.270 --> 00:10:05.690
<v Ira Glass>It's Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:07.170
<v Mrs. Mattoon>Hi, Ira Glass.
<v Mrs. Mattoon>嗨，艾拉·格拉斯。

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:14.080
<v Ira Glass>Mrs. Mattoon, here's why I called you. I want to do a little story on the radio about Duki.
<v Ira Glass>马顿太太，我打电话给您是因为我想在电台做一个关于杜基的小故事。

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:14.920
<v Mrs. Mattoon>Duki?
<v Mrs. Mattoon>杜基？

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:27.400
<v Ira Glass>Duki. And I contacted your daughter, Ashley. And she said that for me to book Duki onto my radio show, I was going to first need to contact Yona Lu.
<v Ira Glass>杜基。我联系了您的女儿艾什莉，她说如果要让杜基上我的广播节目，我得先联系约娜·陆。

00:10:27.400 --> 00:10:29.680
<v Mrs. Mattoon>Yeah, you would need to do that.
<v Mrs. Mattoon>是啊，你是得那样做。

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:31.380
<v Ira Glass>And that I needed to do that through you.
<v Ira Glass>而且必须通过您来联系。

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:32.970
<v Mrs. Mattoon>Yeah.
<v Mrs. Mattoon>对。

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:33.980
<v Ira Glass>Who is Yona Lu?
<v Ira Glass>约娜·陆是谁？

00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:48.290
<v Mrs. Mattoon>Yona Lu is a hedgehog. She's basically taken charge of Duki's financial affairs. And I presume this has something to do with money?
<v Mrs. Mattoon>约娜·陆是一只刺猬。她基本上接管了杜基的财务事务。我猜这跟钱有关？

00:10:48.290 --> 00:10:50.980
<v Ira Glass>Well, I don't know actually. I mean, we--
<v Ira Glass>嗯，其实我也不清楚。我是说，我们——

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:53.800
<v Mrs. Mattoon>That's probably why she said to contact Yona Lu.
<v Mrs. Mattoon>那大概就是为什么她说要联系约娜·陆。

00:10:53.800 --> 00:11:03.170
<v Ira Glass>Well, so what do I do now? I was told to contact you if I wanted to get in touch with Yona Lu in order to book Duki. What do I do next?
<v Ira Glass>好吧，那我接下来该怎么办？有人告诉我如果想联系约娜·陆来预约杜基，得找您。我接下来该做什么？

00:11:03.170 --> 00:11:05.800
<v Mrs. Mattoon>Book Duki, OK. You're going to book Duki.
<v Mrs. Mattoon>预约杜基，好的。你要预约杜基。

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:09.230
<v Ira Glass>That's the whole idea. I want to book Duki for the show, for an interview.
<v Ira Glass>没错，就是这个意思。我想预约杜基上节目，接受一次采访。

00:11:09.230 --> 00:11:16.550
<v Mrs. Mattoon>Well, I'll just talk to Yona Lu about it. She says OK, it's OK.
<v Mrs. Mattoon>嗯，我会跟约娜·陆谈谈的。她说行就行。

00:11:16.550 --> 00:11:20.120
<v Ira Glass>Will Yona Lu want to discuss terms or something?
<v Ira Glass>约娜·陆会想要谈条件之类的吗？

00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:23.400
<v Mrs. Mattoon>She doesn't talk.
<v Mrs. Mattoon>她不会说话。

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:27.440
<v Ira Glass>So what's going to happen? All right, should I call you back?
<v Ira Glass>那会发生什么呢？好的，那我应该再打给您吗？

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:31.480
<v Mrs. Mattoon>You could call me back. Or I just go in and check.
<v Mrs. Mattoon>你可以再打给我。或者我就进去看一下。

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:32.730
<v Ira Glass>You'll just go in and check?
<v Ira Glass>您进去看一下？

00:11:32.730 --> 00:11:32.900
<v Mrs. Mattoon>Yeah.
<v Mrs. Mattoon>是啊。

00:11:32.900 --> 00:11:33.540
<v Ira Glass>Should I wait?
<v Ira Glass>我该等着吗？

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:33.930
<v Mrs. Mattoon>Yeah.
<v Mrs. Mattoon>是啊。

00:11:33.930 --> 00:11:46.850
<v Ira Glass>All right, I'll wait.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，我等着。

00:11:46.850 --> 00:11:48.530
<v Mrs. Mattoon>Ira, this is just radio?
<v Mrs. Mattoon>艾拉，这只是广播吧？

00:11:48.530 --> 00:11:49.270
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:11:49.270 --> 00:11:50.500
<v Mrs. Mattoon>Not TV?
<v Mrs. Mattoon>不是电视？

00:11:50.500 --> 00:11:53.050
<v Ira Glass>It's just radio.
<v Ira Glass>只是广播。

00:11:53.050 --> 00:11:58.130
<v Mrs. Mattoon>And nobody is going to get to be on TV?
<v Mrs. Mattoon>没人能上电视吗？

00:11:58.130 --> 00:12:01.520
<v Ira Glass>No, no one's going to be on TV. No, it's strictly radio.
<v Ira Glass>不，没人上电视。不，纯粹是广播。

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:04.510
<v Mrs. Mattoon>OK, Yona Lu doesn't care what happens then.
<v Mrs. Mattoon>好的，那约娜·陆就不介意会发生什么了。

00:12:04.510 --> 00:12:05.890
<v Ira Glass>What if it were TV?
<v Ira Glass>如果是在电视上呢？

00:12:05.890 --> 00:12:19.710
<v Mrs. Mattoon>I think she'd want to be on too. Radio doesn't do much for her because she doesn't talk.
<v Mrs. Mattoon>我想她也会想出镜的。广播对她没什么意义，因为她不会说话。

00:12:19.710 --> 00:12:28.800
<v Ira Glass>All right, as you might imagine, not everybody in the family takes all this so lightly. Danielle's father was never too keen on this.
<v Ira Glass>好了，你可以想象，并不是家里每个人都对这一切轻描淡写。丹妮尔的父亲从来都不太赞成这些。

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:34.760
<v Mrs. Mattoon>He was quite actually bothered by the whole. He thought we maybe had a problem in the family.
<v Mrs. Mattoon>他实际上对这一切非常困扰。他认为我们家里可能出了什么问题。

00:12:34.760 --> 00:12:35.070
<v Ira Glass>Really?
<v Ira Glass>真的吗？

00:12:35.070 --> 00:12:44.130
<v Mrs. Mattoon>Mm-hmm. I mean, for a while there, we had two daughters that only communicated through a duck.
<v Mrs. Mattoon>嗯。我是说，有一段时间，我们家里有两个女儿只能通过一只鸭子进行交流。

00:12:44.130 --> 00:12:47.270
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, that period that you're describing, when do you mean?
<v Ira Glass>是啊，你描述的那个时期，是指什么时候？

00:12:47.270 --> 00:12:50.210
<v Mrs. Mattoon>I would say they maybe were 10 and 12, or 9 and 11.
<v Mrs. Mattoon>我想她们大概10岁和12岁，或者9岁和11岁的时候。

00:12:50.210 --> 00:12:53.280
<v Ira Glass>And they would only communicate through the duck?
<v Ira Glass>她们真的只通过那只鸭子交流？

00:12:53.280 --> 00:13:13.400
<v Mrs. Mattoon>Well, Danielle didn't pay a whole lot of attention to Ashley, but she paid quite a lot of attention to the duck. So if Ashley wanted to get Danielle's attention, all she had to do was rev up the duck.
<v Mrs. Mattoon>嗯，丹妮尔不太理睬艾什莉，但她非常关注那只鸭子。所以如果艾什莉想引起丹妮尔的注意，她只需要把杜基搬出来就行了。

00:13:13.400 --> 00:13:18.490
<v Ira Glass>And how long did this last?
<v Ira Glass>这持续了多久？

00:13:18.490 --> 00:13:30.300
<v Mrs. Mattoon>I can't remember. She would also make Danielle laugh that way. Danielle thought Duki was very funny, but I can't remember her thinking Ashley was funny.
<v Mrs. Mattoon>我不记得了。她还会用那种方式逗丹妮尔笑。丹妮尔觉得杜基很搞笑，但我记不得她觉得艾什莉也很搞笑。

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:56.960
<v Danielle>In terms of the relationship between my sister and me, I don't know why-- I mean, this is probably completely really sick. But I have so much genuine affection and love for Duki that it's very easy to demonstrate those feelings in a way that it's not as easy to demonstrate those feelings toward my sister, just because we never got in the habit of it.
<v Danielle>关于我和我妹妹之间的关系，我不知道为什么——我是说，这可能真的很病态。但我对杜基有非常真挚的爱和喜爱，以至于表现出这些感情很容易，但以同样的方式对我的妹妹表现出这些感情就不那么容易，只是因为我们从未养成这种习惯。

00:13:56.960 --> 00:14:02.170
<v Ira Glass>What percentage of your relationship with your sister is based on your relationship with Duki?
<v Ira Glass>你和你妹妹之间关系的多少比例是基于你和杜基的关系的？

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:32.840
<v Danielle>Well, the really fun part of it is based on my relationship with Duki, but I think as we've gotten older and older, we've gotten more and more self-conscious about the Duki factor in our relationship. But I think a big chunk. I mean, it definitely gives me this vision into her brain that I wouldn't have otherwise.
<v Danielle>嗯，其中真正有趣的部分是基于我和杜基的关系。但我认为随着我们越来越大，我们对人际关系中的“杜基因素”感到越来越不自在。但我认为占了很大一部分。我是说，它确实让我能够进入她的思维，否则我无法做到这一点。

00:14:32.840 --> 00:14:54.480
<v Ira Glass>Well, I did finally snag an interview with Duki. You know, big network radio program that we are, we could pull off this coup, snag for you this interview with an imaginary hand puppet. Anyway, I did make this happen by calling Ashley.
<v Ira Glass>好了，我最终还是拿下了对杜基的采访。你知道，作为我们这样的大型网络广播节目，我们能完成这次政变，为你搞到这次对一个虚构手偶的采访。总之，我通过给艾什莉打电话促成了这件事。

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:56.900
<v Ira Glass>Is Duki still up for this?
<v Ira Glass>杜基还愿意吗？

00:14:56.900 --> 00:15:01.390
<v Ashley>Yeah, he just got back from a party though.
<v Ashley>可以，不过他刚从一个聚会上回来。

00:15:01.390 --> 00:15:02.370
<v Ira Glass>He just got back from a party?
<v Ira Glass>他刚从聚会上回来？

00:15:02.370 --> 00:15:14.230
<v Ashley>Yeah, he was at a happy hour thing with a lot of college students. He's not in college, but he's in a band, so a lot of his friends go to this happy hour on Friday night.
<v Ashley>是啊，他和一群大学生去参加了“欢乐时光”活动。他虽然不是大学生，但他组了个乐队，所以他很多朋友周五晚上都会去参加这个活动。

00:15:14.230 --> 00:15:16.990
<v Ira Glass>All right, well, could you get him?
<v Ira Glass>好的，那你能叫他一下吗？

00:15:16.990 --> 00:15:22.370
<v Ashley>Sure, he's upstairs. Just a sec. Here he is.
<v Ashley>没问题，他在楼上。稍等。他来了。

00:15:22.370 --> 00:15:23.280
<v Ira Glass>Hey, Duki?
<v Ira Glass>嘿，杜基？

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:25.850
<v Duki>Yeah, hey, Ira. How are you doing?
<v Duki>嘿，艾拉。你怎么样？

00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:26.840
<v Ira Glass>I'm just fine.
<v Ira Glass>我很好。

00:15:26.840 --> 00:15:27.870
<v Duki>Long time no see.
<v Duki>好久不见。

00:15:27.870 --> 00:15:31.920
<v Ira Glass>Long time no see back at you. And welcome to our little radio program.
<v Ira Glass>也祝你好久不见。欢迎来到我们的小广播节目。

00:15:31.920 --> 00:15:35.520
<v Duki>So what's going on here? You've got a whole bunch of celebrities on tonight?
<v Duki>所以这是怎么回事？你今晚请了一大堆名人吗？

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:37.940
<v Ira Glass>Well, we actually have a number of different people.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，我们其实请了不少不同的人。

00:15:37.940 --> 00:15:41.790
<v Duki>What about Tom Cruise?
<v Duki>那汤姆·克鲁斯呢？

00:15:41.790 --> 00:15:43.640
<v Ira Glass>They're just like Tom Cruise.
<v Ira Glass>他们就像汤姆·克鲁斯一样。

00:15:43.640 --> 00:15:45.940
<v Duki>OK.
<v Duki>好的。

00:15:45.940 --> 00:16:02.090
<v Ira Glass>Now, Duki, I was talking to Danielle for our radio program, and had her come on and talk about you a little bit. And one of the things that she said was that when she was younger, in order to discipline her if she was doing something that you didn't like, you could pretty much control her with something called nose squeaks?
<v Ira Glass>现在，杜基，我之前为了广播节目和丹妮尔聊过，请她上来聊了聊你。她说的一件事是，当她小时候，如果她做了你不喜欢的事情，你基本上可以用“捏鼻子”来控制她？

00:16:02.090 --> 00:16:17.590
<v Duki>Yeah, because it's a prominent nose. I mean, it sticks out, and you just want to squeak it. Like over Thanksgiving, we were watching The Muppet Show, and Ms. Piggy was on. And she reminded me a lot of Neelie.
<v Duki>是啊，因为她的鼻子很突出。我是说，它很显眼，你就是想捏一下。就像感恩节的时候，我们在看《布偶秀》，猪小姐也在里面。她让我想起了很多妮莉（丹妮尔的昵称）。

00:16:17.590 --> 00:16:17.780
<v Ira Glass>Of Danielle.
<v Ira Glass>是指丹妮尔。

00:16:17.780 --> 00:16:25.470
<v Duki>Mm-hmm. And Kermit told Ms. Piggy, move the pork. And so I was telling Neelie to move the pork all week.
<v Duki>嗯。当时科米蛙对猪小姐说：“把猪肉拿走。”所以那整个星期我都在叫妮莉“把猪肉拿走”。

00:16:25.470 --> 00:16:26.540
<v Ira Glass>And would she move?
<v Ira Glass>她会听吗？

00:16:26.540 --> 00:16:28.950
<v Duki>Yes, she would. She would.
<v Duki>会，她会的。

00:16:28.950 --> 00:16:38.570
<v Ira Glass>Now if Ashley would tell her, if Ashley would sit down on the couch and say to Danielle, "Move the pork," what would the effect of that be?
<v Ira Glass>如果艾什莉告诉她，如果艾什莉坐在沙发上对丹妮尔说“把猪肉拿走”，那会有什么效果？

00:16:38.570 --> 00:16:59.640
<v Duki>You know Neelie. You know how she looks at you when she doesn't approve of something you say or do? She gets this ice-cold stare, and she gives you this sidelong glance that makes you feel like you're about the size of a pea? That's what she does.
<v Duki>你了解妮莉。你知道当她不赞成你说的话或做的事时，她怎么看你吗？她会投来那种冰冷的眼神，还会瞥你一眼，让你觉得你就像豌豆那么大。她就是那样。

00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:08.990
<v Ira Glass>Is there anything about the life of a duck that perhaps you could tell our radio audience that we might not know? I'm sure that you know much more about it than we do.
<v Ira Glass>关于鸭子的生活，有什么你能告诉我们广播听众，而我们可能不知道的吗？我相信你肯定比我们了解得多得多。

00:17:08.990 --> 00:17:15.760
<v Duki>No, not really. I'm kind of an unusual duck. I'm not really in touch with the whole duck scene.
<v Duki>不，没什么特别的。我是只不太一样的鸭子。我其实不太接触鸭子的圈子。

00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:17.800
<v Ira Glass>You're not in touch with the whole scene, yeah.
<v Ira Glass>你不太接触那个圈子，是吧。

00:17:17.800 --> 00:18:53.210
<v Duki>When I had time, I used to migrate once in a while because I had some friends who were ducks. And I tried to keep in touch with them. But lately, I've just started spending more time with people and doing my own thing, and I just don't have time to do those duck things anymore. I just wanted more out of my life than that.
<v Duki>以前有时间的时候，我偶尔会迁徙，因为我有几个鸭子朋友。我试着和她们保持联系。但最近，我开始花更多时间陪人类，做我自己的事情，我就没时间再去干那些鸭子干的事情了。我只是想从生活中得到比那更多的东西。

00:18:53.210 --> 00:20:03.640
<v Ira Glass>Well, the story of a 27-year-old graduate student who talks like a duck naturally brings us to the story of Chickenman. Chickenman first soared the radio airwaves from 1966 to 1969. Nearly everyday, there would be a new episode. These were these short little things, each one two minutes long or so, starting on WCFL here in Chicago, but spreading to over 1,500 radio stations. Three times, by the way, that's three times the number in the public radio network. According to the people who syndicate Chickenman, it has been translated into German, into Dutch, into Swedish. It is still on the air, they say, in several dozen markets, making it one of the longest running radio features anywhere. Chickenman. Chickenman existed years before National Public Radio existed as a national network. Chickenman will continue probably years after we're all gone like the mighty cockroach, like-- I don't know-- like the bagel, like [UNINTELLIGIBLE] Chickenman endures, will endure.
<v Ira Glass>好了，一个27岁、说话像鸭子的研究生的故事，自然让我们想到了鸡侠的故事。鸡侠从1966年到1969年在广播电台风靡一时。几乎每天都有新剧集。这些都是很短的小东西，每集大概两分钟长，最初在芝加哥的WCFL电台播出，后来传播到超过1500个广播电台。顺便说一下，这个数字是公共广播网络数量的三倍。据发行鸡侠的人士称，它已被翻译成德语、荷兰语和瑞典语。他们说它至今仍在几十个市场播出，使其成为历史上播出时间最长的广播节目之一。鸡侠。鸡侠在全国公共广播电台作为一个全国性网络存在之前就已经存在多年了。鸡侠可能会在我们要消失多年后继续存在，像顽强的小强一样，像——我不知道——像百吉饼一样。鸡侠永存，必将永存。

00:20:03.640 --> 00:20:22.130
<v Ira Glass>Well, coming up later in the program, we'll have the first new real Chicken episode since 1969, written especially for This American Life by Dick Orkin, the voice of Chickenman. But first, before that, let's hear what all the fuss was about.
<v Ira Glass>好了，节目稍后，我们将播出1969年以来的第一部真正的鸡侠新剧，这是由鸡侠的配音演员迪克·奥金专门为《美国生活》创作的。但首先，在那之前，让我们听听大家都在忙什么。

00:20:22.130 --> 00:20:34.990
<v Chickenman Announcer>Now another exciting episode in the life of the most fantastic crime fighter the world has ever known, Chickenman.
<v Chickenman Announcer>现在是世界上最了不起的犯罪克星——鸡侠生命中又一段激动人心的插曲。

00:20:34.990 --> 00:20:39.490
<v Women>He's everywhere! He's everywhere!
<v Women>他无处不在！他无处不在！

00:20:39.490 --> 00:21:04.020
<v Chickenman Announcer>Benton Harper, employed as a shoe salesman for a large downtown department store, spends his weekends, his only two days off, striking terror into the hearts of criminals everywhere as the white-winged warrior called Chickenman. How did it come about that Benton Harper, weekend-winged warrior, selected the visage of the chicken in his crusade against the forces of evil? Now, it can be told.
<v Chickenman Announcer>本顿·哈珀，在一家大型市中心百货公司担任鞋类销售员，他利用周末，也就是仅有的两天假，以长着白翅膀的战士——鸡侠的身份，让各地罪犯闻风丧胆。为什么周末战士本顿·哈珀会选择鸡的形象来开启他打击邪恶势力的征程？现在，答案揭晓了。

00:21:04.020 --> 00:21:05.630
<v Saleswoman>Yes, may I help you?
<v Saleswoman>是的，我能为您效劳吗？

00:21:05.630 --> 00:21:08.440
<v Chickenman>How do you do? I'm looking for a costume.
<v Chickenman>你好，我在找一套服装。

00:21:08.440 --> 00:21:09.280
<v Saleswoman>What did you have in mind?
<v Saleswoman>你想找什么样的？

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:12.380
<v Chickenman>Something that will strike terror into the hearts of criminals everywhere.
<v Chickenman>能让各地罪犯闻风丧胆的那种。

00:21:12.380 --> 00:21:14.740
<v Saleswoman>I see, well how about this?
<v Saleswoman>我明白了，嗯，这套怎么样？

00:21:14.740 --> 00:21:15.850
<v Chickenman>No, I don't think so.
<v Chickenman>不，我觉得不太行。

00:21:15.850 --> 00:21:16.990
<v Saleswoman>Why not try it on?
<v Saleswoman>为什么不试穿一下呢？

00:21:16.990 --> 00:21:17.670
<v Chickenman>Very well.
<v Chickenman>好吧。

00:21:17.670 --> 00:21:18.710
<v Saleswoman>Here, I'll help you.
<v Saleswoman>来，我帮你。

00:21:18.710 --> 00:21:19.940
<v Chickenman>Thank you.
<v Chickenman>谢谢。

00:21:19.940 --> 00:21:22.050
<v Saleswoman>There you are. Now take a look in the mirror.
<v Saleswoman>好了。现在照照镜子。

00:21:22.050 --> 00:21:27.160
<v Chickenman>Hmm, not bad. I wonder if you would permit me to conduct a quick experiment outside this store.
<v Chickenman>嗯，还不错。我不知道你是否允许我在店外做一个简单的实验。

00:21:27.160 --> 00:21:34.850
<v Saleswoman>Certainly.
<v Saleswoman>当然可以。

00:21:34.850 --> 00:21:36.300
<v Chickenman>Pardon me, sir.
<v Chickenman>打扰一下，先生。

00:21:36.300 --> 00:21:36.780
<v Man>Yeah?
<v Man>啊？

00:21:36.780 --> 00:21:38.780
<v Chickenman>Are you by chance a vicious criminal?
<v Chickenman>你该不会是个恶贯满盈的罪犯吧？

00:21:38.780 --> 00:21:39.550
<v Man>Uh-uh.
<v Man>呃，不是。

00:21:39.550 --> 00:21:42.040
<v Chickenman>Fine, would you take a look at this costume I'm wearing?
<v Chickenman>很好，你能看看我穿的这身行头吗？

00:21:42.040 --> 00:21:42.410
<v Man>Yeah.
<v Man>啊。

00:21:42.410 --> 00:21:46.590
<v Chickenman>Do you feel anything strange? Anything at all?
<v Chickenman>你觉得有什么奇怪的感觉吗？有什么感觉吗？

00:21:46.590 --> 00:21:47.530
<v Man>Yeah.
<v Man>有。

00:21:47.530 --> 00:21:48.790
<v Chickenman>And what is that?
<v Chickenman>是什么？

00:21:48.790 --> 00:21:51.230
<v Man>I'd like to kiss you.
<v Man>我想亲你一口。

00:21:51.230 --> 00:21:52.100
<v Chickenman>Kiss me?
<v Chickenman>亲我？

00:21:52.100 --> 00:21:52.870
<v Man>Yeah.
<v Man>是啊。

00:21:52.870 --> 00:21:54.330
<v Chickenman>How do you account for that?
<v Chickenman>你怎么解释这个？

00:21:54.330 --> 00:22:02.030
<v Man>Because you look like an adorable bunny rabbit.
<v Man>因为你看起来像只可爱的小白兔。

00:22:02.030 --> 00:22:04.180
<v Saleswoman>Well, how did it go?
<v Saleswoman>好了，情况怎么样？

00:22:04.180 --> 00:22:05.540
<v Chickenman>What else do you have?
<v Chickenman>你还有什么其他的吗？

00:22:05.540 --> 00:22:07.390
<v Saleswoman>A teddy bear and a chicken.
<v Saleswoman>一只泰迪熊和一只鸡。

00:22:07.390 --> 00:22:08.170
<v Chickenman>A teddy bear?
<v Chickenman>泰迪熊？

00:22:08.170 --> 00:22:09.940
<v Saleswoman>It'd be cute.
<v Saleswoman>那会很可爱。

00:22:09.940 --> 00:22:12.680
<v Chickenman>Wrap up the chicken, please.
<v Chickenman>请帮我把那只鸡包起来。

00:22:12.680 --> 00:22:26.780
<v Chickenman Announcer>Be listening tomorrow for another exciting episode in the life of the most fantastic crime fighter the world has ever known, Chickenman.
<v Chickenman Announcer>明天请继续收听世界上最了不起的犯罪克星——鸡侠生命中又一段激动人心的插曲。

00:22:26.780 --> 00:22:31.190
<v Women>He's everywhere! He's everywhere!
<v Women>他无处不在！他无处不在！

00:22:31.190 --> 00:22:50.860
<v Ira Glass>I love these. Do you want to hear another? We have time for another. You want to hear another? The thing I love is how completely low-key the performances are. It's like they're not even trying. It's a complete aesthetic. All right, let's hear one more before we continue with the next act.
<v Ira Glass>我爱这些。你想听另一段吗？我们还有时间听一段。你想听吗？我喜欢的是这些表演完全不紧不慢。就好像他们根本没在刻意表演一样。这是一种完整的美学。好了，让我们在进入下一幕之前再听一段。

00:22:50.860 --> 00:23:04.150
<v Chickenman Announcer>Now, another exciting episode in the life of the most fantastic crime fighter the world has ever known, Chickenman.
<v Chickenman Announcer>现在，世界上最了不起的犯罪克星——鸡侠生命中又一段激动人心的插曲。

00:23:04.150 --> 00:23:08.010
<v Women>He's everywhere! He's everywhere!
<v Women>他无处不在！他无处不在！

00:23:08.010 --> 00:23:12.190
<v Chickenman Announcer>The office of the police commissioner of Midland City.
<v Chickenman Announcer>米德兰市警察局长办公室。

00:23:12.190 --> 00:23:14.600
<v Chickenman Announcer>[PHONE RINGING]
<v Chickenman Announcer>[电话铃响]

00:23:14.600 --> 00:23:16.080
<v Ms. Helfinger>Hello, this is the commissioner's--
<v Ms. Helfinger>喂，这里是局长办公室——

00:23:16.080 --> 00:23:17.730
<v Chickenman>Ms. Helfinger, this is the winged-warrior.
<v Chickenman>海尔芬格女士，我是长翅膀的战士。

00:23:17.730 --> 00:23:18.260
<v Ms. Helfinger>Yes, what is it?
<v Ms. Helfinger>好的，什么事？

00:23:18.260 --> 00:23:21.620
<v Chickenman>Please inform the commissioner that I'm now all set for test sequence number one.
<v Chickenman>请告诉局长，我已经准备好进行一号测试程序了。

00:23:21.620 --> 00:23:22.380
<v Ms. Helfinger>What?
<v Ms. Helfinger>什么？

00:23:22.380 --> 00:23:23.660
<v Chickenman>It's all primed and ready to go.
<v Chickenman>一切准备就绪，可以开始了。

00:23:23.660 --> 00:23:25.590
<v Ms. Helfinger>What are you talking about?
<v Ms. Helfinger>你在说什么？

00:23:25.590 --> 00:23:27.050
<v Chickenman>The chicken missile, Ms. Helfinger.
<v Chickenman>“鸡弹”，海尔芬格女士。

00:23:27.050 --> 00:23:28.160
<v Ms. Helfinger>The chicken missile?
<v Ms. Helfinger>“鸡弹”？

00:23:28.160 --> 00:23:32.400
<v Chickenman>Yes, so tell the commissioner I'm ready for test sequence number one.
<v Chickenman>是的，所以告诉局长我准备好进行一号测试程序了。

00:23:32.400 --> 00:23:33.310
<v Commissioner>Yes, Ms. Helfinger?
<v Commissioner>好的，海尔芬格女士？

00:23:33.310 --> 00:23:36.730
<v Ms. Helfinger>Commissioner, the chicken missile is ready to go.
<v Ms. Helfinger>局长，鸡弹准备好发射了。

00:23:36.730 --> 00:23:37.080
<v Commissioner>Huh?
<v Commissioner>啊？

00:23:37.080 --> 00:23:38.810
<v Ms. Helfinger>The chicken missile.
<v Ms. Helfinger>鸡弹。

00:23:38.810 --> 00:23:40.910
<v Commissioner>Oh, yes of course, the--
<v Commissioner>噢，对，没错，那个——

00:23:40.910 --> 00:23:43.400
<v Ms. Helfinger>And it's ready for test sequence number one.
<v Ms. Helfinger>它已经准备好进行一号测试程序了。

00:23:43.400 --> 00:23:44.600
<v Commissioner>Test sequence number one.
<v Commissioner>一号测试程序。

00:23:44.600 --> 00:23:45.200
<v Ms. Helfinger>Number one.
<v Ms. Helfinger>一号。

00:23:45.200 --> 00:23:49.380
<v Commissioner>Well, that's very nice. Very nice, yes.
<v Commissioner>嗯，那太好了。很好，是的。

00:23:49.380 --> 00:23:50.270
<v Ms. Helfinger>Hello, winged-warrior?
<v Ms. Helfinger>喂，长翅膀的战士？

00:23:50.270 --> 00:23:51.270
<v Chickenman>Right here, Ms. Helfinger.
<v Chickenman>在这儿，海尔芬格女士。

00:23:51.270 --> 00:23:53.470
<v Ms. Helfinger>The commissioner said that's very nice.
<v Ms. Helfinger>局长说那太好了。

00:23:53.470 --> 00:23:57.530
<v Chickenman>Oh fine, in that case, Ms. Helfinger, have the commissioner stand by with the chicken missile receiver.
<v Chickenman>噢，太好了，既然这样，海尔芬格女士，让局长守着鸡弹接收器。

00:23:57.530 --> 00:23:58.330
<v Ms. Helfinger>What?
<v Ms. Helfinger>什么？

00:23:58.330 --> 00:23:58.830
<v Chickenman>I'm going to count down.
<v Chickenman>我要开始倒数了。

00:23:58.830 --> 00:23:59.660
<v Ms. Helfinger>Listen.
<v Ms. Helfinger>听着。

00:23:59.660 --> 00:24:01.730
<v Chickenman>10, 9-- We'll see you at 14:00 hours.
<v Chickenman>10，9——我们14:00点见。

00:24:01.730 --> 00:24:04.850
<v Ms. Helfinger>Hello, wa--
<v Ms. Helfinger>喂，长翅——

00:24:04.850 --> 00:24:05.870
<v Commissioner>Yes, Ms. Helfinger?
<v Commissioner>是的，海尔芬格女士？

00:24:05.870 --> 00:24:10.800
<v Ms. Helfinger>Commissioner, if I would say to you, prepare the chicken missile receiver, would you know?
<v Ms. Helfinger>局长，如果我要对您说，准备好鸡弹接收器，您知道怎么做吗？

00:24:10.800 --> 00:24:11.560
<v Commissioner>No, I wouldn't.
<v Commissioner>不，我不知道。

00:24:11.560 --> 00:24:13.350
<v Ms. Helfinger>I didn't think you would. Commissioner?
<v Ms. Helfinger>我就猜您不知道。局长？

00:24:13.350 --> 00:24:13.880
<v Commissioner>Yes?
<v Commissioner>是的？

00:24:13.880 --> 00:24:16.420
<v Ms. Helfinger>I would suggest that you crouch under your desk.
<v Ms. Helfinger>我建议您蹲在办公桌下面。

00:24:16.420 --> 00:24:17.330
<v Commissioner>Crouch under my desk?
<v Commissioner>蹲在办公桌下面？

00:24:17.330 --> 00:24:19.380
<v Ms. Helfinger>Yes, it should provide some protection.
<v Ms. Helfinger>是的，这应该能提供一点保护。

00:24:19.380 --> 00:24:20.360
<v Commissioner>From--
<v Commissioner>从——

00:24:20.360 --> 00:24:36.500
<v Commissioner>[CRASHING SOUNDS]
<v Commissioner>[撞击声]

00:24:36.500 --> 00:24:36.980
<v Commissioner>--what?
<v Commissioner>——什么？

00:24:36.980 --> 00:24:41.420
<v Ms. Helfinger>From the chicken missile.
<v Ms. Helfinger>从鸡弹那里保护你。

00:24:41.420 --> 00:25:09.850
<v Chickenman Announcer>Wow. say, that chicken missile really works nifty. Will the Midland City fire department recommend that a chicken missile receive be installed in what's left of Midland City Hall? Be listening tomorrow for another exciting episode in the life of the most fantastic crime fighter the world has ever known, Chickenman.
<v Chickenman Announcer>哇，说真的，那枚鸡弹真的很厉害。米德兰市消防局会建议在剩下的米德兰市厅里安装一个鸡弹接收器吗？明天继续收听世界上最了不起的犯罪克星——鸡侠生命中又一段激动人心的插曲。

00:25:09.850 --> 00:25:48.970
<v Women>He's everywhere! He's everywhere!
<v Women>他无处不在！他无处不在！

00:25:48.970 --> 00:27:03.320
<v Ira Glass>Well, coming up, life on a real turkey farm, David Sedaris, and, of course, the first new episode of Chickenman since the Nixon administration, written just for our program. That's all in a minute when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>好了，接下来，火鸡农场的生活，大卫·塞达里斯，当然，还有尼克松政府以来第一部鸡侠新剧，专门为我们节目创作的。所有内容一分钟后继续。

00:27:03.320 --> 00:27:39.150
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a topic, bring you documentaries, short fiction, found tape, anything we can think OF on that theme. Theme today, with the turkey of Thanksgiving just behind us, the turkey of Christmas ahead of us, we're in that turkey sandwich part of the year. And we are in the middle of it. We are at the center of that sandwich. And in honor of that, we're holding our annual poultry slam here on This American Life . This next story is from Julie Showalter, who grew up on a turkey farm in Missouri.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周节目里，我们都会设定一个主题，为你带来纪录片、短篇小说、发现的录音，或者任何我们能想到的关于这个主题的东西。今天的主题是，随着感恩节火鸡刚过去，圣诞节火鸡就在眼前，我们正处在这一年的“火鸡三明治”时期。我们正处于其中。我们正处在那个三明治的中心。为了纪念这一点，我们要在《美国生活》举办一年一度的家禽大满贯。下一个故事来自朱莉·肖沃尔特，她是在密苏里州的一个火鸡农场长大的。

00:27:39.150 --> 00:28:12.320
<v Julie Showalter>The day before the night 3,000 thousand turkeys died, we moved 13,000 turkeys to the range. This requires some explanation. Turkeys spend their first 16 weeks in a heated brooder house. Then, they are put outside to range in fenced enclosures. Usually, men in trucks come and take them to the range at night. This time, daddy decided we'd herd them ourselves.
<v Julie Showalter>在3000只火鸡死掉的前一天晚上，我们把13000只火鸡移到了牧场。这需要解释一下。火鸡前16周是在加热的育雏室里度过的。然后，它们被放到围栏围起来的牧场里。通常，男人们会开车在晚上把它们运到牧场。这一次，爸爸决定让我们自己放牧。

00:28:12.320 --> 00:28:58.830
<v Julie Showalter>It looked simple enough. We made a temporary chute of wire fencing that ran from the double-end doors of the brooder house 50 yards to the pen. We would get behind the turkeys in the brooder house, shout, wave old shirts and gunny sacks at them, and they would run out the doors, through the chute and into the pen. And that's the way it worked in the first brooder house. The first turkeys hesitated at the door, walked out cautiously, then moved through the chute and dispersed. The rest followed. It took about an hour. Daddy was pleased. "Let's work straight through, he said. "We'll be done by 10:00."
<v Julie Showalter>看起来很简单。我们用铁丝围栏做了一个临时滑道，从育雏室的双开门通向50码外的围栏。我们站在育雏室里火鸡的后面，大喊大叫，挥舞旧衬衫和麻袋，它们就会跑出门口，穿过滑道进入围栏。第一个育雏室就是这样操作的。第一批火鸡在门口犹豫了一下，谨慎地走出来，然后穿过滑道散开。剩下的跟了上来。大约花了一个小时。爸爸很高兴。“我们要一鼓作气干完，”他说，“10点前就能干完。”

00:28:58.830 --> 00:29:31.580
<v Julie Showalter>By the time turkeys have been in a brooder house for 16 weeks, the air is filled with ammonia, feather particles and dust. The stench is overwhelming. After an hour in the brooder house, your lungs hurt for a day. You can contract disabling lung diseases from working only a week in a poultry house. Tiny barbed pieces of feather dig into the tissue of your lung and never let go. But we didn't know that then.
<v Julie Showalter>当火鸡在育雏室里呆了16周后，空气中充满了氨气、羽毛碎屑和灰尘。恶臭令人窒息。在育雏室呆上一小时后，肺部会疼上一整天。仅在禽舍工作一周，就可能感染致残的肺病。羽毛上细小的倒刺会扎进肺部组织，永远出不来。但那时我们不知道。

00:29:31.580 --> 00:30:08.660
<v Julie Showalter>We moved the temporary fence to the doors of the second brooder house. When we threw open the doors at the end of the second house, it was 9:00 in the morning. The sun streamed in the open doors on turkeys that had never seen direct sunlight. The one thing you can count on with turkeys is that you never know how they are going to react. I've seen turkeys clamor against a fence trying to get into a range fire. I've seen them rush toward a screaming child trying to kill it, and I've seen them run from a screaming child spooked and terrified.
<v Julie Showalter>我们把临时围栏移到了第二个育雏室的门口。当我们在早上9点打开第二个房子的门时，阳光直射在从未见过直射阳光的火鸡身上。关于火鸡，你唯一能指望的就是你永远不知道它们会有什么反应。我见过火鸡在围栏上争先恐后地想冲进牧场的火堆里。我见过它们冲向尖叫的孩子想杀了他，也见过它们被尖叫的孩子吓得魂飞魄散逃跑。

00:30:08.660 --> 00:30:49.900
<v Julie Showalter>These turkeys didn't want to go into the sun. As we pushed from behind, they compacted. It was like an old adventure movie where the walls are closing in, but there was no wall at the end, only a patch of sunlight, which the turkeys would not touch. We yelled louder, waved our cloths, kicked at the ones in the rear. Finally, Daddy walked through the solid carpet of turkeys to break the logjam at the front. He stood at the edge of the sunlight, lifting the turkeys three or four at a time with his feet, stirring them with his legs, forcing them into the sun.
<v Julie Showalter>这些火鸡不想进阳光里。当我们从后面推时，它们聚在一起。这就像老冒险电影里墙壁向中间靠拢的场景，但尽头没有墙，只有一块它们不敢触碰的阳光。我们更大声地叫喊，挥舞布料，踢后面的火鸡。最后，爸爸穿过密集的火鸡毯，打破了前面的僵局。他站在阳光的边缘，一次用脚拨开三四只火鸡，用腿搅动它们，强行把它们赶进阳光里。

00:30:49.900 --> 00:31:21.960
<v Julie Showalter>Suddenly, they broke free. As stubbornly as they had refused to go into the light, they now rushed toward it. They ran in a panic, piling on top of each other, knocking down the temporary fence. By the time daddy could get the doors closed, at least 1,000 turkeys had escaped and were running free on the farm, onto our neighbor's farm, into the road.
<v Julie Showalter>突然，它们挣脱了束缚。就像之前拒绝进入光里一样执拗，它们现在冲向光。它们惊慌失措地奔跑，挤在一起，撞倒了临时围栏。等爸爸关上门时，至少有1000只火鸡逃跑了，在农场里、邻居家农场里、公路上到处奔跑。

00:31:21.960 --> 00:32:02.680
<v Julie Showalter>We didn't own the turkeys. We raised them for a company that owned the hatchery, the feed mill, the fleet of trucks that delivered and loaded the turkeys, the processing plant. We got a portion of the profits, if there were profits. With 1,000 turkeys gone, there would be no profits on this flock. 16 weeks of daddy working 14-hour days, of my sisters and me working alongside him anytime we weren't in school, all for no pay. And if we weren't paid for this flock, we would have no cash coming in until the next flock was raised.
<v Julie Showalter>这些火鸡不是我们的。我们是为一家拥有孵化场、饲料厂、运载火鸡的卡车车队和加工厂的公司养殖的。如果盈利，我们能分到一部分利润。丢了1000只火鸡，这群鸡就不会有利润了。爸爸16周每天工作14小时，我妹妹们和我只要不上学就和他一起工作，所有这些都是无偿的。如果我们这群鸡拿不到钱，在下一群鸡养大之前，我们将没有任何现金收入。

00:32:02.680 --> 00:32:50.540
<v Julie Showalter>It took us eight hours to round up the escaped turkeys, four of us trying to track down 1,000 birds that had the whole world in which to hide and run from us. The sun beat down, and the air was thick and humid. We stopped once for water, and my sister Billie, the youngest of us, just 11, vomited from the cold water hitting her stomach after hours of sun, heat, and dehydration. As she lay on the ground, shaking and holding her stomach, I hated her for being the one too sick to continue. But even she was not too sick. We all went on. She got an extra five minutes to rest, but we all went on.
<v Julie Showalter>我们花了八个小时才把逃跑的火鸡找回来，我们四个人试图追踪1000只鸟，它们有整个世界可以躲藏和逃离我们。太阳炙烤着，空气潮湿而粘稠。我们停下来喝过一次水，我妹妹比莉，我们中最小的，才11岁，因为在阳光下暴晒、炎热和脱水后喝下冷水而呕吐了。当她躺在地上颤抖、捂着肚子时，我恨她，恨她是那个太虚弱而无法继续下去的人。但即使她也并非真的不行。我们都继续前行。她得到了额外的五分钟休息时间，但我们都坚持了下来。

00:32:50.540 --> 00:33:33.810
<v Julie Showalter>You may be asking right now how my father could be so cruel, how he could work young girls like that. Or you may think I'm exaggerating, that self-pity has magnified our distress. I tell you, this is no exaggeration. And I tell you, my father had no choice. Or any choice he had was so far in the past that there was no unraveling it. Years later, when we were grown, we caught a glimpse of his guilt, his bitterness over what he had done to us. "I couldn't afford niggers," he told my sister Billie, "so I had daughters."
<v Julie Showalter>你现在可能在问，我父亲怎么能这么残忍，怎么能这样奴役幼小的女儿。或者你可能认为我在夸大其词，认为自怜放大了我们的痛苦。我告诉你，这绝非夸张。我也告诉你，我父亲别无选择。或者他拥有的任何选择都已是过去太久的事情，无法挽回了。多年后，当我们长大成人，我们瞥见了他的愧疚和对他对自己所作所为的苦涩。“我请不起黑人，”他告诉妹妹比莉，“所以我有女儿。”

00:33:33.810 --> 00:34:16.040
<v Julie Showalter>At 6 o'clock, we rebuilt the chute. We opened the doors, and the 6,000 remaining turkeys, the sun now low behind them, walked through to the pen. We cleaned up, we ate supper, and we went to bed. That's the day we had before the night 3,000 turkeys died. At midnight, mother woke us up. "We have to get to the pen. Daddy needs us." We had been too exhausted to hear the storm. We ran out in the driving rain. Flashes of lightning showed daddy picking up turkeys and throwing them one after the other.
<v Julie Showalter>6点钟，我们重建了滑道。我们打开大门，剩下的6000只火鸡，太阳现在落在了它们身后，走进了围栏。我们打扫干净，吃了晚饭，去睡觉了。那就是3000只火鸡死掉的前一天。午夜，母亲叫醒了我们。“我们得去围栏那边。爸爸需要我们。”我们太累了，没听见风暴的声音。我们跑进倾盆大雨中。闪电的光芒映照出爸爸抓起火鸡一只接一只地扔出去。

00:34:16.040 --> 00:34:58.760
<v Julie Showalter>When people learn I grew up on a turkey farm, they invariably ask, "Is it true? Are they really so stupid that they open their mouths in the rain, look up at the sky, and drown?" The answer is yes. Some of them do that. They are that stupid. But that's not how 3,000 die in one night. They die because they are scared, and they huddle together in their fear. They climb on top of each other trying to get close, to find protection in the mass of bodies. And they suffocate. We called it piling.
<v Julie Showalter>当人们知道我是在火鸡农场长大的时，他们总是问：“这是真的吗？它们真的蠢到在大雨中张开嘴看着天空把自己淹死吗？”答案是肯定的。有些确实会那样。它们就是那么蠢。但那并不是3000只鸡一晚上死掉的原因。它们死是因为它们害怕，它们在恐惧中挤在一起。它们试图爬到对方身上以求靠近，试图在尸体堆中寻求保护。然后它们窒息了。我们称之为“堆叠”。

00:34:58.760 --> 00:35:43.430
<v Julie Showalter>It wasn't unusual for a loud noise to cause a pile in the brooder house. If there wasn't someone to pull them off each other, 50 could die because someone slammed a door. But this was worse than any pile we'd seen. Turkeys who'd never spent a night outdoors, panicked by thunder and lightning and rain in sheets. All we could do was pull them out of the pile and throw them away from it. They would run back, still seeking the comfort of the group. After a while, standing in mud, grabbing soaked turkeys, throwing them, grabbing some more, you don't know if the ones you are throwing are dead or alive. You don't care. Maybe we saved some.
<v Julie Showalter>育雏室里因为巨大的噪音引发堆叠并不罕见。如果没有人把它们拉开，因为有人砰地关上门，50只鸡就可能死掉。但这次比我们见过的任何一次堆叠都严重。火鸡从未在户外过夜，被雷电和倾盆大雨吓坏了。我们能做的只是把它们从堆叠中拉出来，扔到远处。它们又跑回来，仍在寻求群体的安慰。过了一会儿，站在泥里，抓着湿透的火鸡，扔掉它们，再抓一些，你不知道你扔的那些是死是活。你不在乎。也许我们救了一些。

00:35:43.430 --> 00:36:31.760
<v Julie Showalter>The next day, the sky was cloudless, and the sun bore down on us again. We picked up dead turkeys, throwing them onto the back of a flatbed truck. Daddy drove the truck into a field far from the house. 3,000 dead turkeys sitting in the Missouri sun for two days. The company that owned the turkeys had them insured against acts of God, so we couldn't do anything until the insurance inspector had seen them. He came, a man from town in a white shirt and tie who held his handkerchief over his face when he got close to the truck. He made no pretense of counting, just stood there gagging.
<v Julie Showalter>第二天，天空万里无云，太阳又炙烤着我们。我们捡起死掉的火鸡，扔到平板卡车的后面。爸爸把车开到离房子很远的田野里。3000只死火鸡在密苏里州的阳光下暴晒了两天。拥有这些火鸡的公司给它们买了保险，防范天灾，所以我们在保险检查员看到它们之前什么也做不了。他来了，一个从镇上来的穿白衬衫系领带的男人，走近卡车时捂着鼻子。他没有假装数数，只是站在那里干呕。

00:36:31.760 --> 00:36:47.160
<v Julie Showalter>After he left, Daddy shoveled the turkeys into Dead Turkey Gulch. He poured gasoline on them and struck a match. They burned for days.
<v Julie Showalter>他走后，爸爸把火鸡铲进“死火鸡峡谷”。他浇上汽油，划了根火柴。它们烧了几天。

00:36:47.160 --> 00:37:30.520
<v Ira Glass>Julie Showalter lives in the Chicago area. She says she still eats turkey twice a year. She's just finished a novel called Needlework and is looking for a publisher.
<v Ira Glass>朱莉·肖沃尔特住在芝加哥地区。她说她一年还是吃两次火鸡。她刚完成一部名为《针线活》的小说，正在寻找出版商。

00:37:30.520 --> 00:37:42.440
<v Ira Glass>Well, David Sedaris is a commentator on NPR'S Morning Edition , the author of the book, Barrel Fever and the forthcoming book, Naked. And he contributed the following story to our poultry slam. It's an excerpt from his diaries.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·塞达里斯是美国国家公共电台《早间版》的评论员，也是《桶装热病》的作者，以及即将出版的《赤身裸体》一书的作者。他为我们的“家禽大满贯”贡献了下面这个故事。这是他日记的节选。

00:37:42.440 --> 00:38:20.430
<v David Sedaris>July 19, 1992. This afternoon at the 26th Street flea market, I had one of those experiences that remind me why I shop in the first place. Not shop like grocery shop, but step out into the world searching for that one thing I cannot name. I pass the usual objects, the grinning mammies offering themselves up as salt and pepper shakers, the coffee table made from dice, another head carved from a coconut. "That's collectible," the dealers say, referring to an ashtray in the shape of a doll-sized toilet bowl. Collectible to who?
<v David Sedaris>1992年7月19日。今天下午在第26街跳蚤市场，我有了那种让我记起当初为什么去购物的经历。不是那种去杂货店买东西，而是走进世界，去寻找那种我说不出名字的东西。我路过常见的那些物品，那些做成盐和胡椒瓶的咧嘴笑的黑人保姆雕像，用骰子做的咖啡桌，另一个从椰子上雕刻出来的头。“那有收藏价值，”交易商说，指着一个马桶形状的烟灰缸说。对谁有收藏价值？

00:38:20.430 --> 00:39:03.240
<v David Sedaris>Last weekend at the flea market, I saw this thing, a taxidermy turkey's head attached to its own foot. This turkey was equipped with that length of flesh that spills from the top of its beak and fell to its neck. Stiff red hair stood out from the head and shoulders, and the claws were really sharp. You'd think that something armed like that might be able to protect itself. I pictured its maker standing by a chopping block saying, "I know. I'll take the turkey's head and attach it to the foot." Why would you do that? And more importantly, what sort of life would you lead that might enable you to make this connection?
<v David Sedaris>上周末在跳蚤市场，我看到了这个东西，一个动物标本火鸡头，连着它自己的一只脚。这只火鸡装备着那种从鸟喙上方溢出并垂到脖子的肉质长条。僵硬的红发从头部和肩膀上竖起，爪子真的非常锋利。你会觉得拥有这种武装的东西应该能保护自己。我脑海中浮现出它的制作者站在案板旁说：“我知道了。我要把火鸡头割下来连到脚上。”你为什么要那么做？更重要的是，你需要过什么样的生活才能让你做出这种连接？

00:39:03.240 --> 00:39:43.260
<v David Sedaris>I was hypnotized by this object and asked the price as if I were under a spell. "$45," the dealer said. My tongue was dry from hanging in the open air, and I tried to fit it back into my mouth. "All right," she said, "35, 30." But she could have gone up. "All right, 85, 120, 370." I had no choice but to follow wherever she led me. I handed over my wallet in a trance, just gave it to her, thinking she could take the whole thing, the cash, blank checks, library card, whatever. Take it all. I stared into the face of this taxidermied turkey's head and nothing else mattered.
<v David Sedaris>我被这个东西催眠了，问价时就像中了咒语一样。“45美元，”交易商说。我的舌头因为一直张着嘴而发干，我试着把它缩回嘴里。“好吧，”她说，“35，30。”但她本可以涨价的。“好吧，85，120，370。”我别无选择，只能跟着她指引的方向走。我如痴如醉地交出钱包，直接给了她，心想她可以把一切都拿走，现金、空白支票、借书证，什么都行。全部拿走吧。我凝视着这个动物标本火鸡头的脸，除此之外什么都不重要。

00:39:43.260 --> 00:40:14.370
<v David Sedaris>Tomorrow, what's that? Yesterday doesn't count. My life began the moment I could call this thing my own. On the way home, I felt giddy and confident that I could approach anyone at all and say, "I'll give you 100, no $500,000 if you can guess what I've got in this paper sack." And I swear that not one of them could have come up with the right answer. I walked home 30 blocks, looking everyone square in the eye and thinking, "Sucker."
<v David Sedaris>明天？那是什么？昨天不算数。我的生活从我能称这个东西为我有物的那一刻开始。回家的路上，我感到头晕目眩，自信满满，我可以走过去对任何人说：“只要你能猜出我这纸袋里装的是什么，我给你100块，不，50万美元。”我发誓他们中没一个人能猜对。我走了30个街区回家，直视每个人的眼睛，心想：“傻瓜。”

00:40:14.370 --> 00:40:27.740
<v Ira Glass>David Sedaris lives in New York City. The stage adaptation of his SantaLand Diaries is now at the Atlantic Theater in Manhattan.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·塞达里斯住在纽约。他的《圣兰日记》舞台改编版目前正在曼哈顿的大西洋剧院上演。

00:40:27.740 --> 00:40:33.120
<v Wbez Operator>Thank you for calling WBEZ radio. Our offices are closed to observe the holiday. If you know your--
<v Wbez Operator>感谢致电WBEZ电台。我们的办公室现已关闭以庆祝假期。如果您知道您的——

